{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant navigating complex political landscapes and frequent conflicts. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic efforts to maintain Tripoli's independence amidst competing powers like the Zengids the Crusaders and the burgeoning Mamluk Sultanate.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He inherited the County upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were characterized by consolidating his authority within the County dealing with internal factions and asserting Tripoli's position within the Crusader states. He faced challenges from rebellious vassals and sought to strengthen the county's defenses and economy. Raymond II had worked to establish Tripoli as a vital trading center and Raymond III continued this policy fostering relationships with merchants from various regions.\n\nRaymond III’s reign witnessed significant military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against the Zengids a powerful Muslim dynasty controlling much of Syria. He fought to maintain Tripoli’s strategic importance and prevent Zengid expansion. His military leadership was tested repeatedly resulting in both victories and setbacks. He also dealt with threats from other Crusader states particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His diplomacy was crucial in navigating these rivalries and securing alliances when necessary. He participated in Crusades including the Third Crusade though his involvement was less prominent than some other Crusader leaders.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his skillful diplomacy. He sought to balance relationships with various powers including the Byzantine Empire the Holy Roman Empire and the various Muslim rulers in the region. He formed alliances strategically to protect Tripoli’s interests. His relationships with the Zengid rulers were particularly complex involving periods of conflict and negotiation. He understood the importance of maintaining a delicate balance of power to ensure Tripoli’s survival. He also worked to attract settlers and merchants to the County bolstering its economy and population.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1199. His death marked a transition in the County of Tripoli. His successors faced increasing pressure from the Mamluk Sultanate which steadily gained power in the region. While Raymond III successfully defended Tripoli for many years his reign ultimately could not prevent the eventual loss of the County to the Mamluks in 1289. He is remembered as a capable ruler a skilled diplomat and a resilient defender of the Crusader states in the Levant. His reign represents a crucial period in the history of Tripoli demonstrating the challenges and complexities of ruling a small Crusader state in a volatile region. His efforts to maintain independence and prosperity left a lasting impact on the County’s development.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.629365427419543e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.4318398573086597e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.00208470341